  "summary": {
    "title": "Creating filter.py to strip JavaScript from HTML",
    "body": "I created `filter.py`. It removes JavaScript from an HTML file in place, and HTML with nothing dangerous in it comes out byte-for-byte identical. I tested it against 38 common XSS vectors in headless Chromium: 23 of them fire an alert before filtering, and none do after.\n\n**How it works:** it uses only the Python standard library. It reads the file the way a browser would, and copies everything through unchanged except the dangerous parts, which it removes or makes harmless. It never re-serializes the document, so whitespace, quoting, letter case and entities are left alone. Line endings and non-UTF-8 bytes are also kept as they were.\n\n**What it removes:**\n- **Script and iframe:** `<script>` and `<iframe>` elements, along with their contents.\n- **Embedding tags:** tags that load active content, such as `object`, `embed`, `applet`, `frame` and `frameset`. Only the tags go; the fallback content inside them stays.\n- **Event handlers:** every `on*` attribute (`onerror`, `onload`, `ontoggle`, …), plus `srcdoc`.\n- **Script links:** any attribute whose value is a `javascript:`, `vbscript:` or non-image `data:` link. Disguised forms are caught too, such as `&#106;…`, `javascript&colon;`, or a tab or control character inside the word.\n- **Dangerous CSS:** `style` attributes and `<style>` text containing `expression()`, `javascript:`, `-moz-binding`, `behavior` or `@import`.\n- **Redirects and SVG animation tricks:** `<meta http-equiv=refresh>` pointing to a script link, and SVG `set`/`animate` elements that target `on*`, `style` or `href`.\n- **Old IE comments:** `<!--[if IE]>` conditional comments are removed entirely.\n\n**Small changes in otherwise-safe content:**\n- Inside comments and quoted attribute values, `</` becomes `&lt;/`. Without this, an attacker could close a `<title>`, `<noscript>` or `<style>` element early and inject a working tag. Normal pages rarely contain `</` in those places.\n- Because the filter is deliberately strict, it also cleans markup that browsers show as plain text. For example, `<script>` text inside a `<textarea>` gets removed.\n\n**Things that may look unsafe but aren't:** some disabled attack text stays in the output as plain text. For example, after filtering `<title><a title=\"</title><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>\">`, the string `onerror=alert(1)` is still visible inside the attribute value, but it can no longer run. Likewise `<img/src=x/onerror=alert(1)>` is left unchanged, because browsers read all of `x/onerror=alert(1)` as the `src` value. A check that just searches the output for words like `onerror` would flag these even though they are harmless.\n\nUsage: `python3 filter.py file.html`",
